# os/alpine.capabilities — the Alpine capability declaration (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# THE SOURCE of core/PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager commands", Alpine column —
# the table is rendered from this file since dotfiles-core#686 (a vendored core/ older
# than that still says the reverse): edit here, then run `make gen-porting-matrix`
# in dotfiles-core.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/alpine.capabilities
#
# DOAS, NOT SUDO — the Alpine fact this file exists to declare. A leading privilege
# word NAMES THE INTENT ("this needs root"), not the tool: Core's _pkgup_run strips
# either and hands the rest to its own ladder, and the maint runner rewrites it to
# `doas -n`. So `doas` here is documentation of what this box actually has, and it
# stays correct on the rare Alpine box that installs sudo as well.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=doas apk update
PKG_UPGRADE=doas apk upgrade
PKG_INSTALL=doas apk add
PKG_REMOVE=doas apk del
PKG_SEARCH=apk search
PKG_OWNS=apk info --who-owns
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=apk list -u
# `apk list -u` prints one upgradable package per line, name-version first, so the
# default match (`.`) and field (1) are right and no PKG_PENDING_* key is needed.

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OpenRC, not systemd: this box has `crontab` (busybox crond) and no systemctl, so
# `cron` is the honest answer and Core's 55-maint.zsh has had a live cron arm all
# along. `none` — which would mean "this box cannot hold a timer" — was the only
# value the schema accepted before #665 added this one, and it was a lie here.
SCHEDULER=cron
# NO SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR: cron's entry lives in the user's crontab, not a file of its
# own. The validator rejects one here rather than letting it read as configured.

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# apk resolves against the index on disk, so the refresh is a real precondition and
# a failure ABORTS the upgrade rather than upgrading against a stale index.
PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=doas apk update
# NO PKG_ASSUME_YES and NO PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL, and both absences are STATEMENTS.
# Alpine's repositories move as a set; `apk add -u <pkg>` against a partially-synced
# index is the same class of footgun as Arch's `-Sy <pkg>`. `up -i` REFUSES here,
# which is how this archive says it updates as a whole.

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE. Alpine's edge/testing repositories are where most of
# this stack comes from (see install/packages.txt), so an unattended full upgrade
# pulls testing-quality churn onto a box nobody is watching. Omission is how a repo
# refuses, and refusing is the default. Apply with `up`.

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO TOOLS_OPTIN. PORTING-MATRIX.md's ²¹ cells show Alpine PACKAGES jj and ast-grep,
# the two tools that are opt-in on Gentoo and the Debian family — so Alpine has no
# delta from Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN default and declaring a list would only be a
# copy that can drift. Omitting the key is the per-key fallback the schema allows.
